Chapter 1: Moonlight Whispers
The moon hung low over the estate, silver spilling across polished floors and soft, warded blankets cradling the children. Tsukihiko, now three, sprawled on the rug, giggling as he attempted to stack blocks that always tumbled over at the last moment. His little grey eyes sparkled in delight, and he babbled in simple Japanese phrases, half-warning, half-laughing: “Otōsan… danger!”
Sukuna lounged nearby, amber eyes gleaming with amusement. One finger tapped lightly against the floor as he watched his son, letting the child’s laughter echo like music through the nursery. “Danger, hmm?” he murmured, voice low and teasing. “Who taught you that, little fox?”
Leora leaned against the window frame, her long, thick white curls spilling down her back like molten silver. Her piercing blue eyes caught the glow of the moon, bright and cold yet softened by the warmth of the room. She tilted her head, watching Sukuna and Tsukihiko, and the corner of her full lips curved into a mischievous smile. “I think he’s been paying attention to someone else’s lessons,” she said softly, her tone teasing, her gaze locking on Sukuna.
“Someone else?” he echoed, stepping closer, letting the shadow of his presence brush against her. He crouched near the blocks, amber eyes locked on hers, and added in a mock-serious whisper, “Do tell… I would be… most displeased if he learned anything inappropriate.”
Leora laughed, a soft, musical sound that made the air between them shimmer. “Oh, don’t flatter yourself. He’s mostly… me.” She reached over, brushing her hand lightly against his arm, lingering just long enough to spark a shiver across his skin. “Though he does pick up a little of your… intensity.”
Sukuna’s grin deepened, playful yet predatory. He leaned closer, amber eyes flickering toward her lips before darting back to her eyes, reading her like an open page. “Intensity, hm? You mean this?” His hand grazed hers, holding it just for a moment, the touch light yet full of weight, the unspoken claim clear.
Tsukihiko let out a small squeal, tugging at Sukuna’s sleeve. “Otōsan! Eat!” he said, holding up a block as if it were a weapon.
Sukuna’s grin softened. He lifted the boy effortlessly, placing him on his lap, spinning him gently. “Ah, yes. Very well. I shall eat, but only if I am careful not to spill,” he murmured, voice a teasing purr, letting Tsukihiko giggle against his chest.
Leora shook her head, her smile bittersweet. Even now, watching Sukuna with their son, she felt the familiar thrill of being seen, of being chosen—not as a vessel, not as a tool, but as a partner, a mother, and a woman who was desired. Her fingers brushed against the hair of Mizuki, the infant in his cradle, who yawned sleepily, amber eyes catching the faint glow of Sukuna’s energy.
“And the twins?” Sukuna asked, voice softer now, eyes flicking toward the small cradles.
“They’re fine,” Leora said, smoothing Satsuki’s blanket. Grey eyes met hers briefly, calm and knowing beyond her months, a tiny mirror of the family’s bloodline. “Satsuki watched Tsukihiko play. Mizuki… is taking a nap, dreaming of some trouble I imagine.”
Sukuna chuckled low in his throat. He crossed to the cradles, kneeling beside them, and brushed a finger along Mizuki’s tiny hand. “Dreams of mischief, I see. You’ve inherited your father’s charm, little one,” he murmured. The infant stirred, amber eyes fluttering open, and gave a soft gurgle.
Leora’s gaze softened. “And yet, your curse of a father will ensure they inherit something more dangerous than charm,” she teased, eyes gleaming with affection.
Sukuna’s lips twitched in a grin, leaning closer, breath brushing her ear. “Ah… dangerous. I like the sound of that. But,” he added, voice dropping into a whisper filled with amusement and heat, “it’s far more thrilling to see how dangerous you can be, isn’t it?”
Leora shivered at the whisper, fingers tightening just slightly on the window ledge. “Mmm… yes,” she breathed, eyes bright, daring him, challenging him—but letting herself melt in the moment, the playfulness and the intimacy mingling.
Outside, the estate seemed calm, bathed in moonlight, yet the night itself held secrets. Wards shimmered faintly, ever-watchful, because this family—their bond, their children—was not meant to exist in a world that would have torn them apart long ago.
And yet here they were: laughing, teasing, playful, utterly entwined, even in the face of the shadows that waited beyond the walls.
 
