Chapter 2: The Visitor at Dusk
The nursery hummed with quiet life. Tsukihiko leaned against Sukuna’s chest, babbling excitedly in half-formed words, while the twins slept, their small breaths rising and falling like gentle waves. Leora stood by the window, brushing her fingers along her curls as she watched the amber of sunset bleed into silver moonlight.
A sound—footsteps, purposeful and firm—cut through the calm. Sukuna’s senses flared before the body of the visitor even appeared. Leora stiffened but did not move; she recognized the rhythm. A figure emerged from the shadows, tall and deliberate, and the air itself seemed to tense.
“Alexei,” Leora breathed, stepping forward, voice trembling.
Alexei paused, eyes flicking first to Sukuna, then to Leora, before the weight of recognition hit him. “Leora…” he whispered, and the word was thick with longing, fear, and a memory of shared survival. He crossed the threshold carefully, as if stepping into sacred territory, and knelt—or would have, if not for Sukuna’s imposing presence—an unspoken act of respect.
Sukuna rose from the rug, amber eyes narrowing, sensing the tension coiling around the man. He did not move to attack—yet—but the subtle shift in the air made it clear: his patience had limits.
Leora stepped between them, hand lightly on Sukuna’s arm, her gaze steady. “Alexei came to speak, Sukuna. Give him that,” she said, her voice gentle but firm. “He… deserves that much.”
Alexei’s eyes met Sukuna’s, unwavering. “I come with respect, not defiance,” he said, voice low but carrying the weight of his fear. “And with truth.”
Sukuna’s lips pressed together, and for a moment, the amber of his gaze softened, flickering with curiosity and suspicion. “Truth?” he repeated, each word a challenge. “Speak it, then. I have little patience for long preambles.”
Alexei nodded, swallowing hard. “If I don’t take your wife…” His gaze flicked to Leora, who stood taut yet trembling. “…then my own wife will suffer a fate worse than death. Tortured. Broken. And they will not give her the mercy to end it. You understand what I mean, Sukuna.”
Sukuna’s hand twitched, a subtle reminder of the power coiled within him, but he held it back. “And if I were in your position?” he asked, voice low, dangerous, yet measured.
“You’d act,” Alexei said without hesitation. “Exactly as I must. You’d protect her. You’d do what you had to—” He swallowed, voice cracking. “…to save her from them.”
Leora’s breath caught. She stepped forward impulsively, closing the small gap between them. “Alexei,” she whispered, voice trembling. “I—” Her chest heaved as tears welled. She reached him, grasped his arms, and the two of them collided in a fevered hug, desperate, trembling. Memories of stolen moments, childhood protection, and shared scars mingled in the embrace.
Sukuna watched, amber eyes narrowing, a storm of sensation coiling in his chest. Regret. Jealousy. Recognition. He saw how deeply she loved Alexei, how fiercely he had protected her even from a distance. And he understood, with a weight that pressed against his dominance, that he might have to kill this man—this man who saw her as a person, not a vessel, who would do anything to keep her alive.
Alexei pulled back slightly, letting the hug linger a heartbeat longer than propriety allowed, then stepped back, eyes locking on Sukuna. “I know what you are,” he said, voice steady despite the emotion. “I do not challenge you. I do not wish to. But I cannot let them claim her. Not if it means…” He trailed off, and Leora shivered at the unspoken horrors.
Sukuna’s voice cut through the silence, low and laced with amusement, danger, and unyielding possession. “You care for her,” he stated, more than asked. His amber eyes flicked to Leora, and then back to Alexei. “You would give everything to keep her safe. And yet… you have brought your hand to my threshold.”
“I bring only warning,” Alexei said, unwavering. “And a request. A chance for reason. For survival. Leora is not a tool. She is not theirs to claim. She is alive, and she is herself. If I fail to act… I cannot forgive myself. You understand that, yes?”
Sukuna’s lips twitched into a faint, almost imperceptible smirk, eyes calculating. The tension in the room was taut, a line stretched over shadowed steel. And yet, in that moment, he glimpsed the truth Alexei carried: fierce, unyielding, protective love—not of him, but of her.
Leora stepped closer to Sukuna, her hand brushing his chest. “I trust you,” she murmured, though her eyes flickered between the two men. “But… listen to him. He knows me. And he… he speaks for my life, too.”
Sukuna’s grin returned, slow and measured, dangerous, amber eyes glittering like fire in moonlight. “Ah… trust. And yet, someone else dares to speak for what belongs to me.” He glanced at Alexei, voice dropping to a near whisper: “I will not be taken unawares.”
Alexei held his gaze, resolute. “Then hear me, Sukuna. Protect what you can. I do not seek conflict, but if it comes… if I must intervene to keep her alive… know that I would do it without hesitation.”
The air thickened with unspoken warnings, mutual respect, and the weight of impossible choices. Leora’s fingers tightened slightly on Sukuna’s chest, anchoring herself to both him and the precarious balance of the night.
Tsukihiko clapped his hands and babbled happily, breaking the tension, unaware of the adult stakes surrounding him. Mizuki gurgled in his sleep, amber eyes fluttering open. Satsuki’s grey gaze seemed to linger on the adults, calm, curious, already absorbing the unspoken gravity of the moment.
Sukuna’s hand brushed a curl from Leora’s face, amber eyes flicking once more to Alexei. “Very well,” he said finally, voice low and deadly calm. “You speak for her life. I will… listen. But remember this—if you misstep…” His gaze sharpened like a blade. “…I will see it, and I will not forgive.”
Alexei nodded, the unspoken agreement settling between them, and for the briefest moment, the three adults held a fragile truce: a mutual understanding of stakes, emotions, and the impossibility of the situation.
Leora exhaled softly, leaning back against the window frame, and whispered to Sukuna, a teasing glint in her eye despite the tension: “See? Even your enemies love me.”
Sukuna’s grin was slow, dangerous, and satisfied all at once. “They do,” he murmured, voice low, amber eyes flickering toward her with a heat reserved only for her. “And yet… only I possess you fully.”

 
