Chapter 3: The Weight of Chains
The children had been settled.
Tsukihiko clung sleepily to Sukuna’s shoulder before finally being coaxed into his bedding, his small fingers reluctant to let go even as his eyes fluttered closed. Mizuki had quieted after a brief stir, his amber gaze unfocused but intense even in infancy. And Satsuki—silent, watchful—had not cried at all, her grey eyes lingering too long on the adults before finally closing.
Leora lingered in the doorway longer than necessary.
Sukuna noticed.
Of course he did.
Her hand rested lightly against the frame, her posture composed, but there was something taut beneath it—something coiled and listening.
Alexei noticed it too.
“She always did that,” he said quietly, not looking at her. “When she thought something was coming.”
Leora exhaled softly, almost a laugh, though there was no humor in it. “And I was always right.”
Sukuna’s gaze flicked between them, something unreadable passing behind his eyes.
Then, with deliberate calm, he turned away.
“Come,” he said to Alexei.
It was not a request.
________________________________________
They stepped out into the night.
The estate stretched wide beneath the silver of the moon, wards humming low beneath the surface like a living thing. The air was still—too still—and even the insects had quieted.
Sukuna walked ahead, unhurried, knowing Alexei would follow.
He stopped only when the nursery lights were dim behind them, when Leora’s presence was distant enough that her voice could not carry.
Then he turned.
The shift was immediate.
Gone was the teasing edge, the domestic warmth.
What remained was something ancient. Calculating. Sharp.
“Speak,” Sukuna said.
Alexei did not hesitate.
“The next one won’t ask.”
Silence followed.
Heavy.
Sukuna tilted his head slightly. “No,” he said after a moment. “He wouldn’t.”
Alexei’s eyes sharpened. “So you do feel him.”
A faint smile touched Sukuna’s lips.
“I feel everything that enters my territory.”
A beat.
Then, softer—
“But this one… is different.”
Alexei nodded once.
“Yes.”
He stepped closer, folding his arms, his voice lowering—not out of fear, but out of respect for the weight of what he was about to say.
“He’s not like the others you’ve dealt with. Not like the tribesmen you’ve killed. Not like me.”
Sukuna said nothing.
So Alexei continued.
“He’s a binder.”
That word settled differently.
Sukuna’s gaze sharpened—not with surprise, but with interest.
“A rare thing,” he murmured.
“An old thing,” Alexei corrected. “Older than most techniques still practiced. Older than your era, even, in the way it’s used now.”
Sukuna’s smile widened slightly.
“Then explain it to me.”
Alexei exhaled slowly.
“The collars,” he said, lifting his hand to his throat—not touching, not quite. “You’ve seen them, haven’t you?”
“I’ve felt them,” Sukuna replied. “On others. Never close enough to study.”
“They are not restraints,” Alexei said. “Not in the way you think. They don’t just suppress. They define.”
Sukuna’s brow lifted slightly.
“Define.”
“They take what you are,” Alexei said, voice tightening, “and they carve it into something usable.”
A pause.
“They bind your will to another’s command. Not fully—not like mindless puppetry. That would be inefficient. No… they leave you aware. Thinking. Feeling.”
His jaw clenched.
“They let you understand what you’re doing.”
Sukuna’s eyes gleamed.
“Cruel.”
“Yes.”
Alexei met his gaze evenly.
“They are modeled after something older. Something not entirely human in origin.”
Sukuna’s interest sharpened further.
“Go on.”
Alexei hesitated only a fraction.
“Think of it like this,” he said. “A spirit bound by a limiter. Something vast, dangerous… forced into a smaller shape. Controlled by a simple mechanism.”
Sukuna’s grin widened.
“Ah.”
Understanding flickered.
“A leash.”
“Yes,” Alexei said quietly. “But one that can be tightened. Or loosened. Or… twisted.”
His voice dropped further.
“The one who holds authority over the collar doesn’t just suppress you. They can command you. Directly. Through cursed speech. Through ritual bindings layered into the construct.”
Sukuna’s eyes narrowed slightly.
“So the voice carries weight beyond its own technique.”
“Exactly.”
Alexei stepped closer.
“If he tells me to kneel, I kneel. If he tells me to kill, I kill.”
A beat.
“If he tells me to stand still while someone dies in front of me… I stand still.”
The words hung between them like a blade.
Sukuna’s expression did not change—but something in the air did.
A subtle shift.
A recognition.
“Who holds yours?” Sukuna asked.
Alexei didn’t answer immediately.
Then—
“The Gegner line.”
Of course.
Sukuna’s gaze flickered, something cold settling into place.
“And the one who comes now,” he said, voice quieter, “he is of that line.”
“Yes.”
Alexei held his gaze.
“And he is the one who can bring you to your knees.”
The words should have been a threat.
They weren’t.
They were a fact.
Sukuna’s smile returned—slow, dangerous, intrigued.
“I would like to see him try.”
Alexei’s expression didn’t change.
“He won’t try,” he said. “He’ll do it.”
Silence.
Then—
“He doesn’t fight the way you do,” Alexei continued. “He doesn’t overwhelm. He doesn’t clash. He doesn’t announce himself.”
A pause.
“He binds.”
Sukuna’s fingers flexed once at his side.
“How?”
Alexei inhaled slowly.
“He doesn’t need proximity in the way others do. Not once he has a thread. Not once he has a target.”
Sukuna’s gaze sharpened.
“Leora.”
“Yes.”
The word landed like a stone.
“He grew up with her,” Alexei said. “He knows her rhythm. Her energy. The shape of her cursed signature.”
Another pause.
“And she knows him.”
Sukuna’s expression stilled.
Something darker slipped beneath the surface.
“…Explain.”
Alexei didn’t soften it.
“She won’t panic immediately,” he said. “Not the way someone else would. She’ll hesitate.”
A beat.
“She understands him.”
That… struck deeper than anything else.
Sukuna’s eyes darkened—not with rage, but with something sharper.
Possessive.
Calculating.
“Does she love him?”
Alexei held his gaze.
“No.”
But then—
“She could understand why someone would.”
Silence again.
Heavy. Dangerous.
Sukuna turned slightly, looking back toward the estate, toward the faint glow of the nursery.
When he spoke again, his voice was quieter.
“Can the collar bind her?”
Alexei’s answer came without hesitation.
“Yes.”
Sukuna’s gaze snapped back to him.
“To what extent.”
Alexei swallowed.
“If he gets a full bind—if he anchors it properly—he could override anything.”
A pause.
“Even you.”
The air went still.
Truly still.
For a long moment, Sukuna said nothing.
Then—
“…Sever?”
Alexei hesitated.
Then nodded.
“Yes.”
That word did something.
Not outwardly.
But something shifted.
Because Sukuna understood what that meant.
Not just control.
Separation.
Isolation.
Removal.
Leora—cut from him.
His fingers curled slowly.
“Then he dies,” Sukuna said.
Simple.
Certain.
Alexei exhaled.
“If it were that easy, I wouldn’t be here.”
Sukuna’s gaze flicked to him again.
“No,” he agreed. “You wouldn’t.”
A pause.
Then, quieter—
“You love your wife.”
Alexei didn’t look away.
“Yes.”
“And you would trade mine to save her.”
The words were not accusatory.
They were… observational.
Alexei’s voice broke slightly.
“I would do anything to keep her from that fate.”
A beat.
“…You would too.”
Sukuna didn’t answer.
He didn’t need to.
Because they both knew the truth of that.
The silence stretched.
Then Alexei spoke again, softer now.
“I didn’t come to take Leora,” he said. “Not like this. Not from you.”
A breath.
“I came because the one who follows me won’t care what you want.”
Sukuna’s gaze flickered once more toward the estate.
Toward her.
“…When?”
Alexei’s answer was immediate.
“Soon.”
A beat.
“Too soon.”
The wind shifted.
Just slightly.
And for the first time that night—
Sukuna felt it.
Not a presence.
Not yet.
But the absence of something.
A space where something would be.
His smile returned.
Slow.
Sharp.
Interested.
“Good,” he said softly.
Alexei frowned slightly.
“That’s not—”
“I was beginning to grow bored,” Sukuna continued, eyes gleaming faintly in the dark.
But then—
Quieter.
Colder.
“…Now I’m not.”
________________________________________
Inside, unseen—
Leora stood in the doorway.
Listening.
Her hand pressed lightly to her chest.
Because something in the night…
Had shifted.
And she knew—
The next time it moved—
It wouldn’t knock.
 
