The courtyard was quiet—too quiet. A rare stillness had settled over the estate after a week of laughter, teasing, and the endless warmth of small hands clutching Higan.

Leora had stepped back into the main house for a moment, leaving Tsukihiko to cradle the child beneath the afternoon sun that filtered lazily through the high walls. Chōbei lounged nearby, half asleep on the stone steps, and Tōma was sharpening a blade idly, watching the scene with that familiar mix of calculation and impatience. Friedhelm leaned against the fountain, hands tucked into his coat pockets, idly scanning the horizon.

Then the presence came.

The gates groaned on their hinges, slow and deliberate, announcing it before the figure appeared. Choso stepped forward, arms crossed, calm and unhurried, his gaze fixed straight ahead. He did not hurry. He did not flinch at the estate’s imposing walls. He did not smile.

He stopped at the edge of the courtyard, letting the space between him and the family stretch, letting the walls and the distance amplify his presence.

Sukuna had come out without Leora, drawn by instinct more than concern, his steps quiet but precise. He noticed the posture immediately—the composure that demanded attention, the lack of hesitation, the awareness that radiated authority without force.

Choso’s eyes scanned the courtyard, briefly acknowledging Friedhelm leaning against the fountain. There was a flicker—recognition—but he did not speak. Not yet.

Then his gaze caught the child.

Tsukihiko held Higan close, careful, almost reverent. The sun glinted off the child’s hair, a small halo of light around the soft curve of his cheeks.

Choso’s expression shifted ever so slightly. A long, slow pause followed. And then, finally, he spoke.

“…That’s not just a child.”

The words hung across the courtyard, deliberate, blunt. A tension snapped into the air, unseen but undeniable.

Tsukihiko stiffened.

Chōbei was the first to move. He stepped in front of Higan instinctively, one arm out, not aggressively, but protective.

Tōma followed, stepping slightly ahead of Tsukihiko and aligning himself with Chōbei’s stance. His movement was subtle, measured, and precise. He did not challenge Choso, but he created a barrier of sight, a silent message: We are not afraid. We are not passive.

Higan stirred but remained cradled against Tsukihiko, oblivious to the tension growing across the stones.

Friedhelm shifted from the fountain to a position slightly closer, not interrupting, merely observing, a faint weight of curiosity in his posture. His eyes never left Choso, noting the careful composure, the taut restraint, the calculation.

Choso’s gaze swept over the small formation—the two older boys creating a shield, Friedhelm standing watch, the child protected. He did not falter. He did not step forward. But the intensity in his eyes deepened, a recognition settling in like iron.

“…I need to speak with him,” Choso said again. This time, more clearly. His words carried across the courtyard like steel. “Him.”

Every eye followed his line—everyone understood what he meant.

Sukuna’s lips pressed into a thin line. There was no immediate reaction outwardly, but the faint tightening in his jaw betrayed curiosity, irritation, and something deeper: a pull toward the child he had not expected anyone else to sense so acutely.

Leora appeared then, leaning slightly from the doorway, watching her husband’s face carefully. Higan shifted slightly in Tsukihiko’s arms, raising small hands in sleepy reflex.

Chōbei’s stance did not waver. Tōma’s position was steady, precise. They held the line without hesitation.

Friedhelm finally took a small step closer to Choso—not threatening, not imitating aggression. Just present. The subtle gravity of his calm acceptance seemed to register with Choso, though he did not change his posture.

“…He is like the Death Paintings,” Choso said, almost under his breath now, the tension in his tone folding into curiosity and recognition.

The courtyard was silent.

Not one child spoke. Not a single adult interrupted. Only the faint rustle of fabric and the occasional shuffle of feet marked the pause.

Sukuna studied the scene, internalizing it. He noted Tōma’s initiative, Chōbei’s instinctive protection, Tsukihiko’s careful hold. He noted Leora’s quiet watch, Friedhelm’s composed observation. And, of course, the child, radiant and innocent, yet charged with a pull that even Sukuna felt like an undertow in his chest.

Something had shifted, quietly but undeniably.

Choso’s eyes returned to Sukuna’s. Calm. Blunt. Certain.

“…I have a request,” he said.

And in that instant, the quiet estate braced itself, for nothing here would remain the same.

